course: dictionary
lesson: 6307
title: Morse code and Morse the name
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Morse is a word with two jobs. Morse code is a way of sending messages with short and long signals, called dots and dashes. «Morse code writes each letter as a pattern of short and long signals, called dots and dashes.» The word code tells you it is a set of rules that turns letters into signals.
explain: Morse is also a name. Samuel Morse was a real person who helped make the telegraph and the code. So when Morse is a person's name, it starts with a capital letter, like any name.
explain: Context tells you which meaning is right. «In Morse code the distress signal SOS is three dots, three dashes, three dots.» Morse is followed by code and signals, so here it names the code, not a person.
fact: Morse code uses dots (short signals) and dashes (long signals) for each letter.
fact: Samuel Morse helped make the telegraph and the code.
fact: SOS in Morse is three dots, three dashes, three dots, a call for help.
fact: Morse code helped send messages along wires by the telegraph.
fact: Morse is a family name as well as the name of the code.
exercise: «Morse code writes each letter as a pattern of short and long signals.» What are the short and long signals called? | Pings and pongs | Commas and full stops | Capitals and small letters | *Dots and dashes
exercise: «In Morse code the distress signal SOS is three dots, three dashes, three dots.» What does SOS mean? | A sign for a shop | *A call for help | A list of numbers | A hello to a friend
exercise: Who was Samuel Morse? | A flashing light on a ship | A kind of telegraph wire | *A person who helped make the telegraph and the code | A letter of the alphabet
test: «A message became pulses of electric current in a wire, written in Morse code as short and long signals.» What is Morse code made of? | Long words only | *Short and long signals, dots and dashes | Numbers from one to ten | Pictures of boats
test: Ella reads «Morse» on a card. Which meaning fits «Morse code»? | A name of a person who lived long ago | A petrol station | *A way of sending messages with dots and dashes | A type of dreich Scottish weather
exam: In «Morse code», what does code tell you about Morse? | It is a sort of weather | It is a person's name | It means the same as sad | *It is a set of rules for turning letters into signals
